[AN ESPRESSO "MARKED" OR "STAINED"
WITH A SMALL SPLASH OF FOAMED MILK ON TOP.]
[A SWEET CARAMEL LATTE
WITH NICE LAYERS—
PERFECT FOR MELLOW COFFEE LOVERS.]

667
00:23:25,053 --> 00:23:25,353
[AN ESPRESSO "MARKED" OR "STAINED"
WITH A SMALL SPLASH OF FOAMED MILK ON TOP.]
[A SWEET CARAMEL LATTE
WITH NICE LAYERS—
PERFECT FOR MELLOW COFFEE LOVERS.]

668
00:23:25,755 --> 00:23:27,699
Even though there’s also “macchiato,”
which she doesn’t know the meaning of.

669
00:23:27,718 --> 00:23:28,248
Yes.

670
00:23:28,280 --> 00:23:30,002
And Bonnie ordered Cacao Nibs Cold Brew.

671
00:23:30,051 --> 00:23:34,370
It has cacao and chocolate
mixed with coffee.

672
00:23:34,522 --> 00:23:37,881
Cacao nibs have a lot of benefits.

673
00:23:37,924 --> 00:23:39,191
Is it cocoa?

674
00:23:39,222 --> 00:23:41,069
-It’s a bit bitter.
-Same source, so it’s bitter.

675
00:23:41,121 --> 00:23:41,571
Yes.

676
00:23:41,591 --> 00:23:43,705
As it hasn’t been mixed with sugar yet.

677
00:23:44,201 --> 00:23:45,148
Meanwhile, Hin.

678
00:23:45,370 --> 00:23:47,400
He ordered all the desserts here.

679
00:23:48,177 --> 00:23:52,102
He said,
"Go ahead and order. I'll help you."

680
00:23:52,244 --> 00:23:54,008
Who else calls him “Hin”?

681
00:23:54,097 --> 00:23:55,896
Tay, New, Off, and Gun.

682
00:23:56,628 --> 00:23:58,251
This one is good. It’s different.

683
00:23:58,447 --> 00:24:03,314
There’s mochi filling with matcha cake,
with a cookie-like texture.

684
00:24:03,410 --> 00:24:05,696
It’s a cookie.

685
00:24:05,807 --> 00:24:07,499
Who’s more of a dessert lover?

686
00:24:07,536 --> 00:24:08,139
-Me.
-P'Mi.

687
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:09,500
Emi and New. Oh?

688
00:24:11,909 --> 00:24:13,244
They’re so alike.

689
00:24:13,681 --> 00:24:14,540
What's with team dividing?

690
00:24:14,567 --> 00:24:17,194
It really feels like Team A and Team B.

691
00:24:17,207 --> 00:24:17,850
[TEAM A vs TEAM B]

692
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:23,925
-What flavor is it?
-Mochi?

693
00:24:23,952 --> 00:24:25,149
-Black sesame?
-Mochi.

694
00:24:25,714 --> 00:24:27,143
Is this place famous for its mochi?

695
00:24:30,937 --> 00:24:31,795
Sesame.

696
00:24:32,047 --> 00:24:33,352
Wow, P’Tay, this one isn’t sweet.

697
00:24:33,521 --> 00:24:34,290
Really?

698
00:24:34,453 --> 00:24:35,659
What is this? Cheesecake?

699
00:24:35,685 --> 00:24:36,668
Cheesecake, yes.

700
00:24:36,724 --> 00:24:37,288
It’s good.

701
00:24:37,306 --> 00:24:39,253
Explain it in words.

702
00:24:39,510 --> 00:24:42,419
It has a nice cheesecake aroma
with a soft texture.

703
00:24:42,460 --> 00:24:44,880
There’s a hint of vanilla too.

704
00:24:45,151 --> 00:24:47,508
There you go. Say more.

705
00:24:47,594 --> 00:24:49,988
And this croissant has perfect crispiness.

706
00:24:50,007 --> 00:24:52,277
You can really smell the butter slightly.

707
00:24:52,300 --> 00:24:56,002
I’m going to do a little fusion
using this jam here.

708
00:24:56,025 --> 00:24:57,440
They go really well together.

709
00:24:57,459 --> 00:24:58,192
Try it.

710
00:24:58,657 --> 00:25:00,000
One, two…

711
00:25:00,290 --> 00:25:02,783
You need to make
the train sound, toot toot~

712
00:25:02,807 --> 00:25:03,936
Toot toot~

713
00:25:05,130 --> 00:25:06,491
Chugga chugga chugga chugga…

714
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:10,334
[SWEETNESS OVERLOADED]

715
00:25:10,343 --> 00:25:13,860
This is the trick I always use with P’Mi
when she doesn’t want to eat.

716
00:25:13,887 --> 00:25:15,860
[SAME TRICK]

717
00:25:16,305 --> 00:25:17,868
It’s true.

718
00:25:18,520 --> 00:25:19,967
“I’m full now.”

719
00:25:19,992 --> 00:25:21,588
“Toot toot~”

720
00:25:22,070 --> 00:25:28,986
Yes, use this trick
if your kid refuses to eat.

721
00:25:29,016 --> 00:25:29,737
It really works.

722
00:25:29,813 --> 00:25:32,271
-It works on adults too.
-It does.

723
00:25:32,331 --> 00:25:35,087
Lately, I have less appetite for food.

724
00:25:35,290 --> 00:25:37,899
Yes, I’m full.

725
00:25:38,282 --> 00:25:39,800
Toot toot~ Chugga chugga~

726
00:25:44,908 --> 00:25:45,616
(Chugga chugga too?)

727
00:25:45,980 --> 00:25:47,348
Yes, you need the “chugga chugga”

728
00:25:47,456 --> 00:25:49,697
when the target is hesitant,

729
00:25:49,790 --> 00:25:51,337
like when they’re deciding
whether to eat or not.

730
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:54,902
-What's the principle of this trick?
-Emi must not overreact too much.

731
00:25:56,280 --> 00:25:58,414
-Just a small reaction.
-It does work on her.

732
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:00,232
P’Mi hasn’t really had appetite
for food lately.

733
00:26:00,250 --> 00:26:01,223
I have to use this trick on her.

734
00:26:01,248 --> 00:26:02,700
Do you not feel like eating
because you’re on a diet, or—

735
00:26:02,727 --> 00:26:03,896
Because I’m genuinely full.

736
00:26:03,946 --> 00:26:06,391
Bonnie was like, “You ate so little.”

737
00:26:06,419 --> 00:26:07,511
But I finished one whole box of food.

738
00:26:07,543 --> 00:26:08,897
Bonnie had three.

739
00:26:09,040 --> 00:26:11,096
Then she turned to me and said,
“Eat more.”

740
00:26:11,115 --> 00:26:13,974
-I had to tell her,
“I’m really full. I can’t eat anymore.”
-That’s camera talk.

741
00:26:14,487 --> 00:26:16,400
But when I do “toot toot~,”
she takes another bite.

742
00:26:17,090 --> 00:26:20,164
-I’m the one who gets confused.
-Can you do this trick more than once?

743
00:26:20,210 --> 00:26:20,962
No, just once.

744
00:26:20,982 --> 00:26:23,200
Just once, when she’s caught off guard,

745
00:26:23,234 --> 00:26:25,278
when she’s zoning out,

746
00:26:25,382 --> 00:26:27,702
when she’s hesitating about
whether to eat or not,

747
00:26:27,731 --> 00:26:29,383
after she’s basically done.

748
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:31,028
I’m the type who eats more main dishes.

749
00:26:31,057 --> 00:26:35,896
And he said to me,
“I’m limiting my savory food.”

750
00:26:36,115 --> 00:26:39,691
We once went to a buffet
with premium beef.

751
00:26:39,777 --> 00:26:41,364
They even had scallops and everything.

752
00:26:41,392 --> 00:26:43,991
He barely ate those,
but ordered 20 servings of mochi.

753
00:26:43,991 --> 00:26:44,888
[20X MOCHI]

754
00:26:44,888 --> 00:26:45,930
“Hey, eat the beef!”
[20X MOCHI]

755
00:26:46,260 --> 00:26:48,192
[SWEET TOOTH HIN]

756
00:26:48,214 --> 00:26:49,502
“Why are you eating mochi?”

757
00:26:49,937 --> 00:26:52,004
Are we doing a heart-to-heart show now?

758
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:53,777
P'Mi too.

759
00:26:54,140 --> 00:26:55,402
Bubble tea.

760
00:26:56,629 --> 00:26:57,805
We were in Taipei.

761
00:26:59,670 --> 00:27:02,299
They took us to many great restaurants,
but she didn’t eat their dishes.

762
00:27:02,647 --> 00:27:05,801
In the morning, she said,
“I’m craving bubble tea.”

763
00:27:06,066 --> 00:27:08,081
At lunch, she asked the staff,
“Do you have bubble tea?”

764
00:27:08,110 --> 00:27:10,240
At dinner, she had bubble tea again.
And one more at night.

765
00:27:10,269 --> 00:27:11,614
-She had four cups of bubble tea.
-On repeat?

766
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:13,029
It’s a must when you’re in Taipei, right?

767
00:27:13,057 --> 00:27:14,725
3 days, 4 cups of bubble tea per day.

768
00:27:14,810 --> 00:27:16,998
From then until now, are you still
wide awake from those cups of tea?

769
00:27:20,388 --> 00:27:21,168
Tay.

770
00:27:21,216 --> 00:27:22,737
Seriously, she only had bubble tea.

771
00:27:22,774 --> 00:27:24,752
Look. He’s drinking matcha here too,
and he just had one earlier.

772
00:27:24,770 --> 00:27:27,192
When I have my drink,

773
00:27:27,630 --> 00:27:29,795
other people who want to try it
just take a small sip.

774
00:27:29,970 --> 00:27:32,650
But he goes like he sucks it down
and finishes it so fast.

775
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:34,925
I can’t drink very cold drinks,
so I leave my drinks—

776
00:27:34,958 --> 00:27:36,198
Hey, I don’t like cold drinks either.

777
00:27:38,010 --> 00:27:38,894
(Why?)

778
00:27:41,090 --> 00:27:44,903
-P’Anon, in 10 years,
will we be like their pair?
-It’s too cold in my throat.

779
00:27:45,576 --> 00:27:47,757
I can’t just leave
my drink there too long.

780
00:27:47,791 --> 00:27:49,091
Or he’d be like,
“I thought you didn’t want it.”

781
00:27:49,120 --> 00:27:50,590
Look at him.
[HEART-TO-HEART SHOW INDEED]

782
00:27:51,450 --> 00:27:54,351
P’Mi loves cold drinks with ice.

783
00:27:54,600 --> 00:27:55,976
[THEY’RE SO ALIKE]

784
00:27:55,976 --> 00:27:57,251
It’s just a waste.
[THEY’RE SO ALIKE]

785
00:27:58,155 --> 00:27:59,007
You’ve got a point.

786
00:27:59,023 --> 00:28:00,368
You should drink it while it’s cold.

787
00:28:00,927 --> 00:28:03,000
Wait until I’m about to throw it away,
then you can drink it.

788
00:28:03,583 --> 00:28:05,998
If not, that means I still want it.

789
00:28:06,666 --> 00:28:08,272
Ah, that matcha is actually yours.

790
00:28:08,373 --> 00:28:09,322
Isn’t P’Tha amazing?

791
00:28:09,350 --> 00:28:13,945
He pairs people who are polar opposites.

792
00:28:14,230 --> 00:28:15,699
They like chewing ice the same.

793
00:28:15,814 --> 00:28:16,822
He really has great vision.

794
00:28:16,900 --> 00:28:18,505
Are you two 5 years apart?

795
00:28:18,561 --> 00:28:19,348
6 years.

796
00:28:20,094 --> 00:28:21,574
Almost 6, 5 years and a few months.

797
00:28:21,674 --> 00:28:23,149
We’re the pair with the biggest age gap
in the company.

798
00:28:23,150 --> 00:28:25,045
Yeah, I was wondering
which pair has the biggest age gap.
[MOST AGE GAP PAIR]

799
00:28:25,066 --> 00:28:26,797
We're Rank 1 now.

800
00:28:26,825 --> 00:28:28,229
-Seriously?
-But we’re like same-age friends the most.

801
00:28:28,351 --> 00:28:29,007
Exactly.

802
00:28:29,143 --> 00:28:30,142
Does she get whiny sometimes?

803
00:28:30,162 --> 00:28:32,068
-But you still call her P’, right?
-P’Mi gets whiny easier than me.

804
00:28:32,185 --> 00:28:33,469
No, we're just whiny about
different things
[DIFFERENT TYPES OF WHINES]

805
00:28:33,480 --> 00:28:34,800
What makes her whiny?
[DIFFERENT TYPES OF WHINES]

806
00:28:35,280 --> 00:28:36,801
When her energy’s running low.
[WHEN ENERGY IS DRAINED]

807
00:28:36,820 --> 00:28:38,912
-When she wants to eat and go home.
-I want to go home.

808
00:28:39,005 --> 00:28:40,074
Why are you laughing?

809
00:28:41,783 --> 00:28:43,200
What makes me whiny?

810
00:28:43,345 --> 00:28:46,445
You get whiny over small things.

811
00:28:46,522 --> 00:28:47,405
Like…

812
00:28:48,090 --> 00:28:49,598
When I don’t reply to your chats.

813
00:28:53,207 --> 00:28:55,500
What? This one too?

814
00:28:55,841 --> 00:28:57,324
Hey, you’re like that too, P’New?

815
00:28:57,930 --> 00:28:59,700
This #$(#@_ always leaves me hanging!

816
00:29:00,705 --> 00:29:02,165
(How many calls didn't he answer?)

817
00:29:02,200 --> 00:29:03,407
Over 10 calls.

818
00:29:03,462 --> 00:29:05,740
I’m rarely on my phone.

819
00:29:07,066 --> 00:29:08,955
But I’m not upset about that anymore.

820
00:29:08,970 --> 00:29:14,777
But now that I know
it can make her sulk, I reply.

821
00:29:14,805 --> 00:29:15,976
Not that I’m used to it,

822
00:29:16,001 --> 00:29:18,050
I just understand her habits
with her phone.

823
00:29:18,050 --> 00:29:19,641
YOU ARE ME, I AM YOU

824
00:29:19,650 --> 00:29:24,870
[TAY & BONNIE] / [NEW & EMI]
OBSESSIVE-COMPULSIVE / LET-IT-GO ATTITUDE
DISLIKES THRILL RIDES / RANDOM EXCLAMATIONS
FOOD POSSESSIVE / LOVES SWEETS
NOT A FAN OF COLD DRINKS / CHEWS ICE
SLOW TO REPLY TEXTS / ETC.
HATES BEING TOLD WHAT TO DO / …

825
00:29:24,979 --> 00:29:27,562
Did you two also work a lot
while studying?

826
00:29:27,924 --> 00:29:29,324
In my case, I worked quite a lot.

827
00:29:29,435 --> 00:29:30,727
I studied in Phetburi, right?

828
00:29:31,271 --> 00:29:32,167
Phetburi Road?

829
00:29:33,220 --> 00:29:35,710
No, the province.
[PHETBURI PROVINCE]

830
00:29:36,920 --> 00:29:41,844
I took a van for 100 baht
to Bangkok to film a series.

831
00:29:41,875 --> 00:29:43,082
So you became an actress
while still in school?

832
00:29:43,110 --> 00:29:44,711
Back when I was still an extra.
[EXTRA PART-TIME]

833
00:29:44,739 --> 00:29:46,150
-So you took the van and paid 100 baht.
-Yes, 100 baht,

834
00:29:46,204 --> 00:29:47,472
and my daily wage was 300 baht.

835
00:29:47,493 --> 00:29:48,961
Then I took the return ride—

836
00:29:48,988 --> 00:29:49,611
Another 100 baht.

837
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:52,654
Yes, which left me
with 100 baht for food.

838
00:29:53,525 --> 00:29:55,290
I worked and studied at the same time.

839
00:29:56,133 --> 00:29:57,952
Having to go back and forth
between work and studying,

840
00:29:57,970 --> 00:30:01,365
especially during thesis time,
that must have been exhausting.

841
00:30:01,389 --> 00:30:02,696
Or did you pause your work then?

842
00:30:02,998 --> 00:30:05,122
I've been working since my first year.

843
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:06,331
Even during your thesis?
[SINCE FIRST YEAR IN COLLEGE]

844
00:30:06,350 --> 00:30:10,014
I did everything, selling things online
and whatever I could.

845
00:30:10,071 --> 00:30:12,130
-You’re really hardworking.
-I’ve been working since I was a freshman.

846
00:30:12,155 --> 00:30:13,804
Honestly, it was really tough,

847
00:30:13,874 --> 00:30:18,241
especially dealing with
a lot of documents.

848
00:30:18,278 --> 00:30:20,795
Back then, everything was paper-based.

849
00:30:20,823 --> 00:30:21,441
[PAPER-BASED EVERYTHING]

850
00:30:21,441 --> 00:30:22,910
We always had to print things out.
[PAPER-BASED EVERYTHING]

851
00:30:22,936 --> 00:30:24,080
Yes, we had to print everything.

852
00:30:24,108 --> 00:30:26,872
And printers always gave me headaches.

853
00:30:27,025 --> 00:30:27,971
Because they were slow?

854
00:30:27,995 --> 00:30:31,613
Yes, too slow, sometimes paper jams,
not printing double-sided.

855
00:30:31,630 --> 00:30:33,401
If you didn’t have a printer
at home back then, it was really hard.

856
00:30:33,542 --> 00:30:34,477
We had to go to campus
to use the printing service.

857
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:37,183
-Not everyone had a printer at home.
-Yes, I had to go to campus just to print.

858
00:30:37,210 --> 00:30:40,013
Nowadays, printers in campus are better,

859
00:30:40,042 --> 00:30:42,177
but it’s still more convenient
to have one at home.

860
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:44,718
Ink-tank printers also
help save on ink costs.
[INK TANK PRINTERS SAVE COST ON INK]

861
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:46,616
You can really save on ink
when printing a lot.
[INK TANK PRINTERS SAVE COST ON INK]

862
00:30:46,640 --> 00:30:49,594
And you can print in large volumes.
Printing outside is expensive.

863
00:30:49,611 --> 00:30:51,840
Ink cartridges back then
were really pricey.

864
00:30:51,869 --> 00:30:53,020
Yes, a single cartridge cost a lot.

865
00:30:53,048 --> 00:30:54,770
Every time I had to print something,
it gave me a headache.

866
00:30:54,798 --> 00:30:57,069
I went through so many printers.

867
00:30:57,157 --> 00:31:00,404
If I had the Canon MegaTank
GX4070 back then,

868
00:31:00,490 --> 00:31:01,697
it would’ve been amazing.

869
00:31:02,570 --> 00:31:05,629
Thank you to Emi and Bonnie for today.

870
00:31:05,659 --> 00:31:07,408
Thanks for having us.

871
00:31:07,441 --> 00:31:08,285
We had a great time.

872
00:31:08,314 --> 00:31:09,800
I feel like we’ve gotten even closer.

873
00:31:10,810 --> 00:31:13,139
Yes, closer than those 20 cues
of filming together.

874
00:31:13,160 --> 00:31:14,691
Not even close to just today.
[NOT AS CLOSE AS JUST TODAY]

875
00:31:14,700 --> 00:31:17,617
-Honestly, I never really got to
really talk to Bonnie.
-True, we barely talked before.

876
00:31:17,825 --> 00:31:19,173
Got any projects you want to share?

877
00:31:19,210 --> 00:31:20,457
Please look forward to our series.

878
00:31:20,487 --> 00:31:21,782
It’ll be released within this year.

879
00:31:21,802 --> 00:31:25,194
The series is called Moonshadow.

880
00:31:25,223 --> 00:31:26,348
Who’s the director?

881
00:31:26,450 --> 00:31:29,000
-P’Fon.
-P’Fon, Kanittha Kwunyoo.

882
00:31:29,200 --> 00:31:30,142
A director you keep working with.

883
00:31:30,167 --> 00:31:31,148
Yes, she also directed…

884
00:31:31,180 --> 00:31:32,597
Scarlet Heart too.

885
00:31:33,388 --> 00:31:34,998
She directed U-Prince for me.

886
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:38,090
Really? U-Prince?
[SHE’S IN EVERY ERA]

887
00:31:39,598 --> 00:31:41,091
I watched it.

888
00:31:41,271 --> 00:31:42,512
-You really watched that?
-Yes.

889
00:31:42,600 --> 00:31:44,881
Talking about old shows
and you can remember everything.

890
00:31:44,909 --> 00:31:45,930
Let’s test your memory.

891
00:31:45,951 --> 00:31:47,496
I think you two have good memories.

892
00:31:48,103 --> 00:31:49,897
You're both still young.

893
00:31:49,928 --> 00:31:50,521
Good memory.

894
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:52,709
If you want to print a lot of documents,

895
00:31:52,786 --> 00:31:55,447
with good-quality color prints at a low cost,

896
00:31:55,543 --> 00:31:56,368
what should you use?

897
00:31:56,614 --> 00:31:59,045
Canon MegaTank GX4070.

898
00:31:59,213 --> 00:32:00,897
Who is this printer suitable for?

899
00:32:01,011 --> 00:32:02,677
People who run businesses.

900
00:32:02,717 --> 00:32:04,982
If you want an all-in-one printer
with Wi-Fi and fax,

901
00:32:05,004 --> 00:32:05,810
which model do you recommend?

902
00:32:05,900 --> 00:32:08,650
Canon MegaTank GX4070

903
00:32:09,118 --> 00:32:12,509
And what can this all-in-one printer do?

904
00:32:12,634 --> 00:32:15,725
Print, scan, copy, and fax.

905
00:32:17,062 --> 00:32:19,715
What about connectivity?
What are the connection options?

906
00:32:19,868 --> 00:32:22,297
iOS, Android, and Wi-Fi.

907
00:32:22,693 --> 00:32:23,860
Wow, that really covers everything.

908
00:32:23,878 --> 00:32:24,902
It supports everything.

909
00:32:24,976 --> 00:32:28,401
So we can say
Emi and Bonnie passed the quiz.

910
00:32:28,420 --> 00:32:29,583
[QUIZ PASSED!]

911
00:32:29,602 --> 00:32:31,482
You spoke in sync. We can do that too.

912
00:32:32,614 --> 00:32:34,485
One in a lower voice,
the other in a higher voice.

913
00:32:35,340 --> 00:32:37,001
I was worried we’d go out of sync.

914
00:32:38,426 --> 00:32:39,200
So cute.

915
00:32:39,223 --> 00:32:42,257
So today, we’d like to thank
Canon MegaTank Printer,

916
00:32:42,287 --> 00:32:45,351
as well as Emi and Bonnie
for joining our conversation.

917
00:32:45,610 --> 00:32:47,696
-It was so much fun.
-We talked so much today.

918
00:32:48,034 --> 00:32:49,684
This is the first show
where I talked this much.

919
00:32:49,710 --> 00:32:50,448
-Really?
-So true.

920
00:32:50,477 --> 00:32:50,940
Yes.

921
00:32:50,960 --> 00:32:52,961
I’m really happy.
Bonnie got to make more friends.

922
00:32:52,995 --> 00:32:55,728
We’d also like to thank Ong Tong Khao Soi,

923
00:32:55,909 --> 00:32:57,522
Matcha People,

924
00:32:57,552 --> 00:33:00,054
and Roots at Ari.

925
00:33:00,077 --> 00:33:02,031
Thank you.

926
00:33:02,090 --> 00:33:04,829
Please look forward to
TayNew Meal Date in—we don’t know when,

927
00:33:04,844 --> 00:33:05,948
so please stay tuned.

928
00:33:08,045 --> 00:33:09,284
Thank you Canon.

929
00:33:09,325 --> 00:33:11,804
Choose a quality printer,
choose Canon MEGA TANK

930
00:33:11,866 --> 00:33:13,010
Never Lets You Down

931
00:33:13,714 --> 00:33:15,974
You don’t like being forced
to do things, right?

932
00:33:16,007 --> 00:33:18,271
Not at all. If someone forces me,

933
00:33:18,297 --> 00:33:21,097
I won’t do it sincerely.
I don’t like that.

934
00:33:21,211 --> 00:33:22,177
You’re really like me.

935
00:33:22,200 --> 00:33:23,297
Say it to the camera.

936
00:33:24,570 --> 00:33:25,903
Did you say hi to a younger one.

937
00:33:25,915 --> 00:33:26,613
Hello.

938
00:33:29,625 --> 00:33:31,302
I thought it was a real person!

939
00:33:31,608 --> 00:33:34,607
They said hi back too.

940
00:33:35,598 --> 00:33:36,740
Don’t play along.

941
00:33:36,764 --> 00:33:39,697
No. I tend to forget people
I used to know.

942
00:33:40,457 --> 00:33:41,602
Same as me.

943
00:33:41,660 --> 00:33:43,604
That’s why you always forget
people’s kindness.

944
00:33:43,681 --> 00:33:46,288
No, that’s you, you—

945
00:33:46,742 --> 00:33:48,951
Take it easy.
Is this normal for this show?

946
00:33:49,860 --> 00:33:51,930
Lift it higher.

947
00:33:53,280 --> 00:33:54,382
He planned it.

948
00:33:54,625 --> 00:33:56,382
She’s laughing at us. Sorry.

